\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{All real types in a binary tetrahedral Cayley graph}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{T=2T} be the [24 Hurwitz units](fgap-000K), with
#{\omega=(-1+i+j+k)/2}, and take the inverse-closed generating set
##{S=\{i,-i,j,-j,k,-k,\omega,\bar\omega\}.}
Give each unordered edge #{\{g,gs\}} weight one, counting it once.
There are no loops, the degree is 8, and left multiplication
acts by signed graph symmetries. On #{\mathbb R^T} the Laplacian is
##{(Lf)(g)=\sum_{s\in S}f(gs)-8f(g).}
The underlying group construction is
\citet{Section 11.2, pp. 166-168}{voight2021quaternion}.
The following spectrum and blocks are calculations for this
particular generating set.}

\p{The quotient #{T/Q_8\cong C_3} gives the character
#{\lambda(\omega)=\zeta=e^{2\pi i/3}}, #{\lambda|_{Q_8}=1}.
Let #{\sigma} be the defining two-dimensional complex quaternion
representation, with #{\chi_\sigma(q)=2\operatorname{Re}q}, and
let #{\tau} act by quaternion conjugation on
#{\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb R}\{i,j,k\}}.
Its character is #{4(\operatorname{Re}q)^2-1}.
The 7 conjugacy classes and their characters are
##{\begin{array}{c|rrrrrrr}
q&1&-1&i&\omega&-\omega&\omega^2&-\omega^2\\\hline
|[q]|&1&1&6&4&4&4&4\\
\lambda^j&1&1&1&\zeta^j&\zeta^j&\zeta^{2j}&\zeta^{2j}\\
\sigma\lambda^j&2&-2&0&-\zeta^j&\zeta^j&-\zeta^{2j}&\zeta^{2j}\\
\tau&3&3&-1&0&0&0&0
\end{array}}
with #{j=0,1,2}. The class of #{i} comprises
#{\{\pm i,\pm j,\pm k\}}; each remaining class is determined
by its #{Q_8}-coset and real part. These descriptions follow by
conjugating the explicit Hurwitz units.}

\p{Using the displayed sizes, the character inner products are
#{\delta_{\alpha\beta}} and the sum of squared degrees is
#{3\cdot1^2+3\cdot2^2+3^2=24}. Thus the 7 characters are
irreducible, inequivalent and complete. Squaring sends the first
three classes to #{1,1,-1}, the two #{\pm\omega} classes to
#{[\omega^2]}, and the two #{\pm\omega^2} classes to #{[\omega]}.
Substitution into #{|T|^{-1}\sum_g\chi(g^2)} gives indicators
#{1,0,0} for #{1,\lambda,\lambda^2}, #{-1,0,0} for
#{\sigma,\sigma\lambda,\sigma\lambda^2}, and #{1} for #{\tau}.
For example the numerator for #{\sigma} is
#{2+2-12-16=-24}; for #{\sigma\lambda} it is
#{4-12-8(\zeta+\zeta^2)=0}.
The [real-type bridge](fdip-0051) therefore gives
##{\operatorname{End}_T(\mathbb R^T)
 \cong\mathbb R\times\mathbb C\times M_3(\mathbb R)
       \times\mathbb H\times M_2(\mathbb C).}
Here the regular real multiplicity is #{m_\Xi=\dim_{D_\Xi}W_\Xi}:
a real simple matrix algebra #{M_r(D)} has #{r} column copies of
its defining module, as in \ref{fcap-0042}.}

\p{Fourier decomposition of right translation gives, up to the
chosen dual-basis convention, the block of #{-L},
#{8I-\sum_{s\in S}\pi(s)}. Its adjoint or transpose gives the
same spectra and determinants, since #{S=S^{-1}}.
The [multiplicity matrices](fdip-0050) are as follows; in the table
#{m} is multiplicity, not the mass parameter:
##{\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c}
W\text{ type}&D&m&\dim_{\mathbb R}W&L_\Xi\\\hline
1&\mathbb R&1&1&0\\
\lambda,\bar\lambda&\mathbb C&1&2&-3\\
\tau&\mathbb R&3&3&J_3-11I_3\\
\sigma&\mathbb H&1&4&-9\\
\sigma\lambda,\sigma\bar\lambda&\mathbb C&2&4&\operatorname{diag}(-6,-9)
\end{array}}
Here #{J_3} is the all-ones matrix. For #{\tau}, the 6
#{\pm i,\pm j,\pm k} contribute #{-2I_3}, while
#{\tau(\omega)+\tau(\bar\omega)=J_3-I_3}; the block has
eigenvalues #{8,11,11}. For #{\sigma} the 6 contributions
cancel and #{\sigma(\omega)+\sigma(\bar\omega)=-I_2}, giving
#{9}. For #{\sigma\lambda}, diagonalize #{\sigma(\omega)}
with eigenvalues #{\zeta,\zeta^2}. The twisted adjacency sum has
eigenvalues #{-1,2}, giving #{9,6}. The nonreal linear pair has
adjacency eigenvalue #{6+\zeta+\zeta^2=5}.}

\p{The real sector dimensions are #{1,2,9,4,8}, summing to #{24}.
These eigenvalues belong to #{-L}. Consequently
##{\operatorname{spec}(L)=\{0,-3^{[2]},-6^{[4]},-8^{[3]},-9^{[8]},-11^{[6]}\},}
where brackets denote eigenvalue multiplicity. For a mass squared
#{a>0}, set #{Q=aI-L}; every block of #{-L} receives the same shift and
##{\begin{aligned}
\det_{\mathbb R}Q={}&a(a+3)^2(a+6)^4\\
 &\cdot(a+8)^3(a+9)^8(a+11)^6.
\end{aligned}}
The complex and quaternionic exponents are real dimensions, not
complex degrees copied without descent. The commutant dimension
is #{24}; the symmetric dimension is #{13}, also the number of
inversion orbits in #{T}, since only #{1,-1} are self-inverse.
These are finite graph calculations; the possible physical
interpretations discussed in \ref{fgap-0018} are separate hypotheses.}
